.TH SKIPPERD 8 "August 2026" "Skipper 360 v0.2.2" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
skipperd \- Skipper 360 PTY session supervisor daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B skipperd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B skipperd
is the background daemon service component of \fBSkipper 360\fR.
It manages single-instance PID locking, listens for IPC requests over a Unix Domain Socket (\fB~/.config/skipper360/skipper.sock\fR), allocates pseudo-terminals (PTYs), detects interactive password prompts via regex patterns, and performs secure memory-zeroed password injection from the OS Keyring.

The daemon also features a dynamic configuration file watcher (\fBnotify\fR) that automatically reloads \fBconfig.toml\fR changes in real-time without restarting, and a daily rolling log appender (\fBtracing-appender\fR) with strict POSIX permissions (mode 0700 directory, mode 0600 log files).

.SH SECURITY ARCHITECTURE & HARDENING

.TP
.B Memory Isolation & Alignment
Secret password memory buffers (\fBAlignedSecretBuffer\fR) are strictly allocated with 64-byte cache line alignment (\fB#[repr(align(64))]\fR) to isolate secret data from adjacent memory addresses and prevent CPU hardware prefetcher side-channel leaks.

.TP
.B Hardware Speculation Barriers
Explicit hardware speculation barrier instructions (\fB_mm_lfence()\fR on x86_64, \fBisb sy\fR on ARM64) are executed immediately before and after credential injection to serialize CPU instruction execution pipelines and block speculative load execution.

.TP
.B Physical Cache Line Flushing
The physical CPU cache line containing secret data is evicted using the \fB_mm_clflush\fR instruction immediately after writing to the PTY master device.

.TP
.B Process Dump Protection
Linux \fBprctl(PR_SET_DUMPABLE, 0)\fR is invoked on startup to prevent ptrace attachment, coredumps, and unprivileged process memory inspection. Speculative Store Bypass (\fBPR_SPEC_STORE_BYPASS\fR) is disabled via \fBprctl\fR on x86_64.

.TP
.B IPC Payload Bounding
IPC requests over the Unix Domain Socket are bounded using \fBAsyncReadExt::take(65536)\fR (64 KiB max request size) to prevent memory exhaustion and Denial-of-Service (DoS) attacks.

.TP
.B Memory Zeroing
All secret buffers implement \fBZeroize\fR and are zero-overwritten in RAM upon \fBDrop\fR.

.SH CONFIGURATION FILE SCHEMA (\fBconfig.toml\fR)

The configuration file is located at \fB~/.config/skipper360/config.toml\fR. Example structure:

.RS
.nf
[general]
mode = "standard"        # "standard" or "silent"
timeout_seconds = 30     # Prompt detection timeout
auto_activate = false

[default_credentials]
username = "skjuve"

[[whitelist.entries]]
command = "su"
keyring_key = "whitelist:su"
match_mode = "prefix"   # "prefix" or "exact"
expires_at = 1786000000 # Optional Unix epoch timestamp (TTL)

[patterns]
custom_patterns = [
    "[pP]assword:?",
    "[sS]udo"
]
.fi
.RE

.SH SIGNALS

.TP
.B SIGINT / SIGTERM
Triggers a graceful shutdown of the daemon, closing active IPC listeners, removing the Unix Domain Socket, and releasing the PID lockfile.

.SH SYSTEMD INTEGRATION

The daemon can be managed as a Systemd user service:
.RS
.nf
$ systemctl --user enable --now skipperd.service
$ systemctl --user status skipperd.service
$ systemctl --user restart skipperd.service
.fi
.RE

.SH FILES

.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/config.toml
Main configuration file. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/skipper.sock
Unix Domain Socket for IPC. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/skipper.pid
PID lockfile preventing multiple daemon instances. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/audit.log
Append-only JSON-lines security audit event log. Mode 0600.
.TP
.I ~/.config/skipper360/logs/skipper.log.YYYY-MM-DD
Daily rolling daemon log file. Directory mode 0700, files mode 0600.

